	-> create clusters
		- kind create cluster --name=<cluster_name> --config=<yml_filename>
		- kubectl cluster-info --context kind-<cluster_name>
		- kubectl get nodes
		- kubectl config use-context kind-<cluster_name> : change default cluster
	
	-> namespace, pods, deployment, service
		- kubectl get ns
		- kubectl get pod -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl get pod -n <ns_name> -o wide
		- kubectl create ns <ns_name>
		- kubectl delete ns <ns_name>
		- kubectl run <pod_name> --image=nginx -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl delete pod <pod_name> -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl apply -f namespace.yml
		- kubectl apply -f pod.yml -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl exec -it <pod_name> -n <ns_name> -- bash
		- kubectl describe pod/<pod_name> -n <ns_name>

		
		
		
		
				
** 11/08/26 **

	-> namespace, pods, deployment, service
		- kubectl apply -f deployment.yml -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl get deployment -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl delete deployment <dpl_name> -n <ns_name>
		- kubectl scale deployment <dpl_name> -n <ns_name> --replicas=10
		
		- Deployments and Replicasets does the same work but Deployments is more advanced and flexible like it provides rollout, rollin updates
		
		- Daemonsets is same as Deployment but it always allocates 1 pod to each node and does not support with replicas 
		
		
		
		
		
		
